WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:08.666 --> 00:11.506 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Yellen's
Press Conference June 15,

00:12.026 --> 00:16.846 align:middle
2016 CHAIR YELLEN.

00:16.846 --> 00:18.126 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:18.126 --> 00:21.576 align:middle
Today, the Federal Open Market
Committee maintained the target

00:21.576 --> 00:26.336 align:middle
range for the federal funds
rate at 1/4 to 1/2 percent.

00:27.246 --> 00:31.096 align:middle
This accommodative policy should
support further progress toward

00:31.096 --> 00:32.976 align:middle
our statutory objectives

00:33.466 --> 00:36.096 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

00:37.126 --> 00:40.536 align:middle
Based on the economic outlook,
the Committee continues

00:40.536 --> 00:43.706 align:middle
to anticipate that
gradual increases

00:43.706 --> 00:48.156 align:middle
in the federal funds rate over
time are likely to be consistent

00:48.156 --> 00:50.936 align:middle
with achieving and
maintaining our objectives.

00:51.936 --> 00:55.676 align:middle
However, recent economic
indicators have been mixed,

00:56.056 --> 00:58.476 align:middle
suggesting that our
cautious approach

00:58.476 --> 01:02.116 align:middle
to adjusting monetary
policy remains appropriate.

01:03.146 --> 01:07.116 align:middle
As always, our policy is
not on a preset course,

01:07.536 --> 01:11.316 align:middle
and if the economic outlook
shifts, the appropriate path

01:11.316 --> 01:14.046 align:middle
of policy will shift
correspondingly.

01:14.696 --> 01:17.316 align:middle
I will come back to
our policy decision,

01:18.076 --> 01:21.726 align:middle
but first I will review
recent economic developments

01:21.726 --> 01:22.426 align:middle
and the outlook.

01:23.626 --> 01:27.486 align:middle
Economic growth was
relatively weak late last year

01:27.486 --> 01:28.646 align:middle
and early this year.

01:29.456 --> 01:32.696 align:middle
Some of the factors weighing
on growth were expected.

01:33.216 --> 01:36.166 align:middle
For example, exports
have been soft,

01:36.536 --> 01:39.246 align:middle
reflecting subdued
foreign demand

01:39.596 --> 01:42.346 align:middle
and the earlier appreciation
of the dollar.

01:43.326 --> 01:47.806 align:middle
Also, activity in the energy
sector has obviously been hard

01:47.876 --> 01:52.436 align:middle
hit by the steep drop in
oil prices since mid-2014.

01:52.866 --> 01:57.596 align:middle
But the slowdown in other parts
of the economy was not expected.

01:58.366 --> 02:01.426 align:middle
In particular, business
investment outside

02:01.426 --> 02:05.816 align:middle
of energy was particularly weak
during the winter and appears

02:05.816 --> 02:08.316 align:middle
to have remained
so into the spring.

02:08.636 --> 02:13.976 align:middle
In addition, growth in household
spending slowed noticeably early

02:13.976 --> 02:18.346 align:middle
in the year despite solid
increases in household income

02:18.896 --> 02:21.006 align:middle
as well as relatively
high levels

02:21.006 --> 02:23.006 align:middle
of consumer sentiment
and wealth.

02:23.766 --> 02:26.696 align:middle
Fortunately, the
first-quarter slowdown

02:26.696 --> 02:29.746 align:middle
in household spending appears
to have been temporary;

02:30.446 --> 02:32.976 align:middle
indicators for the
second quarter have

02:32.976 --> 02:36.446 align:middle
so far pointed to
a sizable rebound.

02:37.196 --> 02:41.056 align:middle
This recovery is a key factor
supporting the Committee's

02:41.056 --> 02:45.396 align:middle
expectation that overall
economic activity will expand

02:45.396 --> 02:48.456 align:middle
at a moderate pace over
the next few years.

02:49.606 --> 02:54.236 align:middle
Despite lackluster economic
growth, the job market continued

02:54.236 --> 02:56.186 align:middle
to improve early in the year.

02:57.216 --> 02:58.466 align:middle
During the first quarter,

02:58.466 --> 03:02.426 align:middle
job gains averaged
nearly 200,000 per month,

03:02.996 --> 03:05.676 align:middle
just a bit slower
than last year's pace.

03:06.436 --> 03:10.506 align:middle
And the unemployment rate held
near 5 percent even though

03:10.746 --> 03:13.896 align:middle
notably more people were
actively looking for work.

03:14.776 --> 03:18.016 align:middle
However, more recently
the pace of improvement

03:18.016 --> 03:21.516 align:middle
in the labor market appears
to have slowed markedly.

03:22.176 --> 03:27.076 align:middle
Job gains in April and May are
estimated to have averaged only

03:27.076 --> 03:28.896 align:middle
about 80,000 per month.

03:29.746 --> 03:33.746 align:middle
And while the unemployment rate
fell to 4.7 percent in May,

03:34.236 --> 03:37.726 align:middle
that decline occurred
because fewer people reported

03:38.086 --> 03:40.026 align:middle
that they were actively
seeking work.

03:40.896 --> 03:43.086 align:middle
A broader measure
of unemployment

03:43.376 --> 03:47.686 align:middle
that includes individuals who
want and are available to work

03:48.086 --> 03:51.646 align:middle
but have not searched
recently as well as people

03:51.646 --> 03:53.336 align:middle
who are working part time

03:53.706 --> 03:56.856 align:middle
but would rather work full
time has flattened out.

03:57.506 --> 04:02.196 align:middle
On a more positive note, average
hourly earnings increased 2-1/2

04:02.306 --> 04:04.326 align:middle
percent over the
past 12 months--

04:04.876 --> 04:09.006 align:middle
a bit faster than in earlier
years and a welcome indication

04:09.006 --> 04:12.406 align:middle
that wage growth may
finally be picking up.

04:12.616 --> 04:15.646 align:middle
Although recent labor market
data have, on balance,

04:15.716 --> 04:19.546 align:middle
been disappointing, it's
important not to overreact

04:19.676 --> 04:21.856 align:middle
to one or two monthly readings.

04:22.746 --> 04:24.806 align:middle
The Committee continues
to expect

04:24.806 --> 04:27.196 align:middle
that the labor market
will strengthen further

04:27.476 --> 04:29.066 align:middle
over the next few years.

04:29.826 --> 04:33.526 align:middle
That said, we will be watching
the job market carefully.

04:34.816 --> 04:36.466 align:middle
Ongoing economic growth

04:36.776 --> 04:40.916 align:middle
and an improving labor market
underpin our inflation outlook.

04:41.736 --> 04:44.266 align:middle
Overall consumer
price inflation--

04:44.646 --> 04:46.406 align:middle
as measured by the price index

04:46.406 --> 04:48.826 align:middle
for personal consumption
expenditures--

04:49.486 --> 04:53.186 align:middle
was about 1 percent over the
12 months ending in April,

04:53.796 --> 04:56.236 align:middle
still short of our
2 percent objective.

04:57.116 --> 05:00.366 align:middle
Much of this shortfall
continues to reflect the effects

05:00.366 --> 05:02.966 align:middle
of earlier declines
in energy prices

05:03.406 --> 05:05.296 align:middle
and lower prices for imports.

05:05.926 --> 05:10.046 align:middle
Core inflation, which excludes
energy and food prices,

05:10.326 --> 05:12.726 align:middle
has been running close
to 1-1/2 percent.

05:13.026 --> 05:17.576 align:middle
As the transitory influences
holding down inflation fade,

05:18.056 --> 05:20.546 align:middle
and as the labor market
strengthens further,

05:21.066 --> 05:24.396 align:middle
the Committee expects
inflation to rise to 2 percent

05:24.686 --> 05:26.796 align:middle
over the next two
to three years.

05:27.966 --> 05:32.676 align:middle
Our inflation outlook also rests
importantly on our judgment

05:32.746 --> 05:36.666 align:middle
that longer-run inflation
expectations remain reasonably

05:36.746 --> 05:37.476 align:middle
well anchored.

05:38.456 --> 05:40.586 align:middle
However, we can't
take the stability

05:40.666 --> 05:43.576 align:middle
of longer-run inflation
expectations for granted.

05:44.556 --> 05:46.116 align:middle
While most survey measures

05:46.116 --> 05:49.936 align:middle
of longer-run inflation
expectations show little change,

05:49.936 --> 05:54.916 align:middle
on balance, in recent months,
financial market measures

05:55.146 --> 05:58.126 align:middle
of inflation compensation
have declined.

05:59.106 --> 06:02.376 align:middle
Movements in these
indicators reflect many factors

06:02.736 --> 06:07.186 align:middle
and therefore may not provide
an accurate reading on changes

06:07.186 --> 06:09.176 align:middle
in the inflation expectations

06:09.416 --> 06:12.216 align:middle
that are most relevant
for wages and prices.

06:12.926 --> 06:16.826 align:middle
Nonetheless, in considering
future policy decisions,

06:16.826 --> 06:20.246 align:middle
we will continue to
carefully monitor actual

06:20.246 --> 06:23.496 align:middle
and expected progress
toward our inflation goal.

06:24.636 --> 06:25.686 align:middle
Let me now turn

06:26.276 --> 06:29.686 align:middle
to the individual economic
projections submitted

06:29.776 --> 06:32.286 align:middle
for this meeting by
FOMC participants.

06:33.036 --> 06:36.686 align:middle
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

06:37.066 --> 06:41.446 align:middle
on his or her own view of
appropriate monetary policy,

06:42.026 --> 06:45.646 align:middle
which, in turn, depends on
each person's assessment

06:45.936 --> 06:48.936 align:middle
of the multitude of factors
that shape the outlook.

06:49.636 --> 06:52.076 align:middle
Participants' projections
for growth

06:52.286 --> 06:56.626 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted gross
domestic product are slightly

06:56.626 --> 06:59.936 align:middle
lower in the near term
than the projections made

06:59.936 --> 07:01.836 align:middle
for the March FOMC meeting.

07:02.836 --> 07:06.176 align:middle
The median growth projection
now remains at 2 percent

07:06.176 --> 07:10.936 align:middle
through 2018, in line with
its estimated longer-run rate.

07:12.186 --> 07:15.266 align:middle
The median projection for
the unemployment rate edges

07:15.306 --> 07:21.606 align:middle
down from 4.7 percent at the
end of this year to 4.6 percent

07:21.606 --> 07:26.146 align:middle
in the next two years, somewhat
below the median assessment

07:26.586 --> 07:29.196 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

07:30.146 --> 07:31.466 align:middle
The median path

07:31.466 --> 07:34.836 align:middle
of the unemployment rate is
little changed from March.

07:35.706 --> 07:39.076 align:middle
Finally, the median
inflation projection stands

07:39.076 --> 07:43.606 align:middle
at 1.4 percent this year,
a bit firmer than in March,

07:43.976 --> 07:49.556 align:middle
and then rises to 1.9 percent
next year and 2 percent in 2018.

07:50.856 --> 07:54.116 align:middle
Returning to monetary
policy, as I said,

07:54.406 --> 07:56.626 align:middle
the Committee maintained
its target range

07:56.626 --> 07:57.896 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

07:58.626 --> 08:02.206 align:middle
This decision reflects the
Committee's careful approach

08:02.526 --> 08:06.056 align:middle
in setting monetary policy,
particularly in light

08:06.056 --> 08:09.446 align:middle
of the mixed readings on the
labor market and economic growth

08:09.756 --> 08:11.986 align:middle
that I have discussed as well

08:11.986 --> 08:14.636 align:middle
as continuing below-target
inflation.

08:15.606 --> 08:19.476 align:middle
Proceeding cautiously in raising
our interest rate target will

08:19.706 --> 08:23.516 align:middle
allow us to verify that
economic growth will return

08:23.516 --> 08:27.206 align:middle
to a moderate pace, that the
labor market will strengthen

08:27.206 --> 08:29.896 align:middle
further, and that
inflation will continue

08:29.896 --> 08:32.536 align:middle
to make progress toward
our 2 percent objective.

08:33.376 --> 08:35.606 align:middle
Caution is all the
more appropriate given

08:35.606 --> 08:38.326 align:middle
that short-term interest
rates are still near zero,

08:38.886 --> 08:43.196 align:middle
which means that monetary policy
can more effectively respond

08:43.196 --> 08:47.786 align:middle
to surprisingly strong inflation
pressures in the future

08:48.446 --> 08:51.716 align:middle
than to a weakening labor
market and falling inflation.

08:52.796 --> 08:55.736 align:middle
Although the financial
market stresses that emanated

08:55.736 --> 09:00.366 align:middle
from abroad at the start of this
year have eased, vulnerabilities

09:00.366 --> 09:02.256 align:middle
in the global economy remain.

09:02.256 --> 09:06.276 align:middle
In the current environment
of sluggish global growth,

09:06.736 --> 09:11.556 align:middle
low inflation, and already very
accommodative monetary policy

09:11.866 --> 09:16.116 align:middle
in many advanced economies,
investor perceptions of,

09:16.116 --> 09:19.816 align:middle
and appetite for, risk
can change abruptly.

09:20.846 --> 09:23.086 align:middle
As our statement
notes, we will continue

09:23.086 --> 09:25.586 align:middle
to closely monitor
global economic

09:25.586 --> 09:27.236 align:middle
and financial developments.

09:28.486 --> 09:30.926 align:middle
We continue to expect
that the evolution

09:30.926 --> 09:34.756 align:middle
of the economy will warrant
only gradual increases

09:34.756 --> 09:36.076 align:middle
in the federal funds rate.

09:37.066 --> 09:40.806 align:middle
We expect the rate to remain,
for some time, below levels

09:41.276 --> 09:44.406 align:middle
that are anticipated to
prevail in the longer run

09:45.356 --> 09:47.826 align:middle
because headwinds weighing
on the economy mean

09:47.826 --> 09:49.416 align:middle
that the interest rate needed

09:49.836 --> 09:54.246 align:middle
to keep the economy operating
near its potential is low

09:54.456 --> 09:56.216 align:middle
by historical standards.

09:57.216 --> 10:00.586 align:middle
These headwinds-- which
include developments abroad,

10:01.046 --> 10:05.226 align:middle
subdued household formation,
and meager productivity growth--

10:05.736 --> 10:07.666 align:middle
could persist for some time.

10:08.756 --> 10:14.156 align:middle
But, if they gradually fade over
the next few years as we expect,

10:14.896 --> 10:19.206 align:middle
then the interest rate required
to keep the economy operating

10:19.436 --> 10:23.066 align:middle
at an even keel should
move higher as well.

10:23.906 --> 10:28.006 align:middle
This view is consistent with
participants' projections

10:28.006 --> 10:30.066 align:middle
of appropriate monetary policy.

10:30.846 --> 10:34.896 align:middle
The median projection for the
federal funds rate rises only

10:34.896 --> 10:38.986 align:middle
gradually to 1-1/2 percent
at the end of next year

10:39.456 --> 10:42.726 align:middle
and 2-1/2 percent
by the end of 2018,

10:43.306 --> 10:47.466 align:middle
somewhat below its estimated
longer-run normal level.

10:48.366 --> 10:51.026 align:middle
Although the median federal
funds rate at the end

10:51.026 --> 10:53.466 align:middle
of this year is unchanged
from March,

10:54.016 --> 10:57.706 align:middle
a number of participants
revised down their projections.

10:58.566 --> 11:04.386 align:middle
For 2017 and 2018, the
median projection is 1/4

11:04.386 --> 11:09.476 align:middle
to 1/2 percentage point lower
than in March, roughly in line

11:09.766 --> 11:14.086 align:middle
with the 1/4 percentage
point downward revision made

11:14.086 --> 11:17.936 align:middle
to the estimated longer-run
level of the federal funds rate.

11:18.616 --> 11:21.596 align:middle
As I have noted on
previous occasions,

11:22.136 --> 11:25.106 align:middle
participants' projections
for the federal funds rate,

11:25.506 --> 11:27.176 align:middle
including the median path,

11:27.696 --> 11:30.886 align:middle
are not a fixed plan
for future policy.

11:31.846 --> 11:34.586 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

11:35.506 --> 11:39.026 align:middle
These forecasts represent
participants' individual

11:39.026 --> 11:43.916 align:middle
assessments of appropriate
policy given their projections

11:43.916 --> 11:48.576 align:middle
of economic growth, employment,
inflation, and other factors.

11:49.496 --> 11:54.006 align:middle
However, the economic outlook
is inherently uncertain,

11:54.586 --> 11:56.736 align:middle
so each participant's assessment

11:56.736 --> 12:01.336 align:middle
of appropriate policy is
also necessarily uncertain,

12:01.956 --> 12:06.536 align:middle
especially at longer time
horizons, and will change

12:06.536 --> 12:08.046 align:middle
in response to changes

12:08.046 --> 12:10.986 align:middle
to the economic outlook
and associated risks.

12:11.946 --> 12:14.836 align:middle
Finally, the Committee
will continue its policy

12:15.126 --> 12:20.286 align:middle
of reinvesting proceeds from
maturing Treasury securities

12:20.556 --> 12:22.946 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

12:23.166 --> 12:24.966 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

12:25.516 --> 12:27.796 align:middle
As highlighted in
our policy statement,

12:28.116 --> 12:32.666 align:middle
we anticipate continuing this
policy until normalization

12:32.666 --> 12:36.746 align:middle
of the level of the federal
funds rate is well under way.

12:38.026 --> 12:40.126 align:middle
Maintaining our sizable holdings

12:40.466 --> 12:44.606 align:middle
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

12:44.606 --> 12:47.766 align:middle
financial conditions and
should reduce the risk

12:47.766 --> 12:51.196 align:middle
that we might have to lower
the federal funds rate to zero

12:51.526 --> 12:54.446 align:middle
in the event of a future
large adverse shock.

12:55.046 --> 12:58.386 align:middle
Thank you, I will be happy
to take your questions.

12:58.766 --> 12:59.726 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

13:00.256 --> 13:06.336 align:middle
Thanks very much.

13:06.336 --> 13:08.366 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

13:08.836 --> 13:10.516 align:middle
One of the big uncertainties
hanging

13:10.516 --> 13:12.536 align:middle
over markets right now
is clearly the vote

13:12.536 --> 13:14.626 align:middle
in the United Kingdom next week.

13:14.696 --> 13:18.696 align:middle
How much of a factor was
that in today's decision,

13:18.696 --> 13:22.486 align:middle
relative to the questions
you've elucidated about,

13:22.486 --> 13:25.066 align:middle
the domestic jobs numbers
and inflation data?

13:25.066 --> 13:27.236 align:middle
And could you talk a little
bit about the channels

13:27.236 --> 13:30.076 align:middle
that you think about when you
talk about the potential impact

13:30.076 --> 13:31.856 align:middle
of a Brexit on the U.S. economy?

13:32.056 --> 13:32.336 align:middle
Thank you.

13:32.336 --> 13:33.456 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

13:33.786 --> 13:39.036 align:middle
Well, Brexit, the upcoming
U.K. decision on whether or not

13:39.096 --> 13:43.206 align:middle
to leave the European Union,
is something we discussed,

13:43.206 --> 13:47.146 align:middle
and I think it's fair to say
that it was one of the factors

13:47.426 --> 13:50.846 align:middle
that factored into
today's decisions--

13:50.886 --> 13:54.746 align:middle
clearly this is a very
important decision

13:55.066 --> 13:57.486 align:middle
for the United Kingdom
and for Europe.

13:57.976 --> 14:05.046 align:middle
It is a decision that could
have consequences for economic

14:05.046 --> 14:09.276 align:middle
and financial conditions in
global financial markets.

14:09.596 --> 14:14.486 align:middle
If it does so, it could
have consequences in turn

14:14.486 --> 14:18.636 align:middle
for the U.S. economic
outlook that would be a factor

14:18.636 --> 14:22.106 align:middle
in deciding on the
appropriate path of policy.

14:22.416 --> 14:27.116 align:middle
So it is certainly one of the
uncertainties that we discussed

14:27.116 --> 14:29.786 align:middle
and that factored
into today's decision.

14:31.636 --> 14:32.626 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:33.376 --> 14:36.056 align:middle
Thank you.

14:36.946 --> 14:40.816 align:middle
The Fed's outlook for
rates has come down sharply

14:40.886 --> 14:44.776 align:middle
for 2018 especially, but it's
been coming down gradually

14:44.776 --> 14:48.006 align:middle
over time-- almost a full
percentage point in some cases,

14:48.006 --> 14:49.866 align:middle
compared to a year ago-- and

14:49.866 --> 14:51.876 align:middle
yet the GDP outlook
remains the same.

14:51.876 --> 14:55.036 align:middle
What has happened in,
say, just the past quarter

14:55.486 --> 14:58.046 align:middle
to the Committee's outlook
for rates to bring it

14:58.046 --> 15:01.946 align:middle
down so much for, say, 2018,
where it's now just 2.4 percent

15:02.366 --> 15:04.796 align:middle
and further from the long
run than it was, say,

15:05.116 --> 15:07.756 align:middle
in the prior estimate
that was out there?

15:08.106 --> 15:10.646 align:middle
Has there been a dramatic
change in the Committee's view

15:10.966 --> 15:13.026 align:middle
on the relationship
of GDP to rates?

15:13.026 --> 15:16.146 align:middle
And maybe you could also
explain why the Fed has

15:16.146 --> 15:19.436 align:middle
to keep lowering these rates
and getting that forecast wrong.

15:19.606 --> 15:20.426 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

15:20.726 --> 15:23.776 align:middle
So, as I mentioned in
my opening remarks,

15:24.256 --> 15:27.656 align:middle
there is really a great
deal of uncertainty

15:27.976 --> 15:31.406 align:middle
around each individual's
assessment

15:31.686 --> 15:35.536 align:middle
of the appropriate level
of rates, particularly

15:35.536 --> 15:39.066 align:middle
as we go further out
in the forecast horizon

15:39.286 --> 15:41.476 align:middle
and when we come
to the long term.

15:42.096 --> 15:47.396 align:middle
And I think what we can see,
and what many econometric

15:47.396 --> 15:52.086 align:middle
and other studies show, is that
the so-called neutral rate--

15:52.086 --> 15:57.556 align:middle
namely, the level of the federal
funds rate that is consistent

15:57.616 --> 16:00.956 align:middle
with the economy
growing roughly at trend

16:00.956 --> 16:03.526 align:middle
and operating near
full employment--

16:03.896 --> 16:07.656 align:middle
that rate is quite depressed
by historical standards.

16:07.976 --> 16:10.806 align:middle
Many estimates would
put it, in real

16:10.806 --> 16:14.006 align:middle
or inflation-adjusted
terms, near zero.

16:14.716 --> 16:19.596 align:middle
Now, the path that you see
in the dot plot for rates

16:19.596 --> 16:22.916 align:middle
over time is importantly
influenced--

16:23.196 --> 16:28.906 align:middle
there is accommodation, and
as we achieve our objectives,

16:28.906 --> 16:32.986 align:middle
I think most participants
feel that the accommodation

16:32.986 --> 16:37.316 align:middle
in the current stance of policy
needs to be gradually removed,

16:37.666 --> 16:41.816 align:middle
but a very important
influence in the out years is,

16:41.986 --> 16:44.936 align:middle
what will happen to
that neutral rate

16:44.936 --> 16:48.946 align:middle
that will just keep the economy
operating on an even keel?

16:49.556 --> 16:53.766 align:middle
And I've often in my
statements and remarks talked

16:53.766 --> 17:00.916 align:middle
about headwinds that
reflect lingering effects

17:00.916 --> 17:06.146 align:middle
of the financial crisis; to the
extent that there are headwinds,

17:06.426 --> 17:09.556 align:middle
I think many of us expect

17:09.556 --> 17:12.706 align:middle
that these headwinds would
gradually diminish over time,

17:12.706 --> 17:17.356 align:middle
and that's a reason why you
see the upward path for rates.

17:17.826 --> 17:19.866 align:middle
But there are also
more long-lasting

17:19.866 --> 17:24.766 align:middle
or persistent factors that may
be at work that are holding

17:25.606 --> 17:29.096 align:middle
down the longer-run
level of neutral rates--

17:29.096 --> 17:32.336 align:middle
for example, slow
productivity growth,

17:32.406 --> 17:35.116 align:middle
which is not just
a U.S. phenomenon

17:35.456 --> 17:37.696 align:middle
but a global phenomenon.

17:38.286 --> 17:40.966 align:middle
You know, obviously, there
is a lot of uncertainty

17:40.966 --> 17:43.786 align:middle
about what will happen
to productivity growth,

17:44.176 --> 17:48.296 align:middle
but productivity growth could
stay long for a prolonged time,

17:48.296 --> 17:54.746 align:middle
and we have an aging-- aging
societies in many parts

17:54.746 --> 17:58.116 align:middle
of the world that could
depress this neutral rate.

17:58.386 --> 18:02.496 align:middle
And I think all of us
are involved in a process

18:02.496 --> 18:08.066 align:middle
of constantly reevaluating where
is that neutral rate going,

18:08.066 --> 18:11.436 align:middle
and I think what you
see is a downward shift

18:11.436 --> 18:16.566 align:middle
in that assessment over time,
the sense that maybe more

18:16.566 --> 18:20.146 align:middle
of what's causing this neutral
rate to be low are factors

18:20.176 --> 18:22.356 align:middle
that are not going to
be rapidly disappearing

18:22.356 --> 18:24.406 align:middle
but will be part
of the new normal.

18:24.706 --> 18:28.896 align:middle
Now, you still see an assessment
that that neutral rate will move

18:28.896 --> 18:31.756 align:middle
up somewhat, but it
has been coming down,

18:31.756 --> 18:35.836 align:middle
and I think it continues
to be marked lower.

18:37.056 --> 18:41.596 align:middle
And it is highly uncertain,
for all of the dots.

18:42.206 --> 18:42.626 align:middle
JASON LANGE.

18:42.946 --> 18:45.396 align:middle
Hi, I'm Jason Lange
with Reuters.

18:45.856 --> 18:49.756 align:middle
The median participant forecast
for the fed funds rate for 2017

18:49.756 --> 18:54.186 align:middle
and 2018 came down quite
dramatically, but this stands

18:54.306 --> 18:57.896 align:middle
in contrast with the
2016 meeting forecast.

18:57.976 --> 19:03.176 align:middle
As you mentioned, there were
a number-- actually, six--

19:03.916 --> 19:07.986 align:middle
participants who saw only
one rate hike this year.

19:08.576 --> 19:15.356 align:middle
Can you comment on what it would
take for two rate increases

19:15.406 --> 19:21.046 align:middle
to be a-- the likely or
appropriate policy path?

19:21.546 --> 19:26.576 align:middle
And about this disconnect
between the median view

19:26.576 --> 19:29.796 align:middle
and the view of the, say, the
voting members of the Committee?

19:29.796 --> 19:32.466 align:middle
If there is one, I should add.

19:32.466 --> 19:32.776 align:middle
Thank you.

19:33.016 --> 19:33.756 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

19:33.756 --> 19:39.056 align:middle
Well, I'm not going to comment
on participants versus voters.

19:41.706 --> 19:44.336 align:middle
You know, monetary policy--

19:44.476 --> 19:48.436 align:middle
the Committee feels
that monetary policy--

19:48.766 --> 19:51.046 align:middle
when we are looking
at several years,

19:51.446 --> 19:54.726 align:middle
we should show the
public what the views are

19:55.006 --> 19:58.916 align:middle
of all the participants in the
Committee, especially given

19:58.916 --> 20:03.046 align:middle
that voting-- voting
rotates every year,

20:03.046 --> 20:06.316 align:middle
and so that's a decision
we made.

20:07.156 --> 20:10.386 align:middle
But you asked me what it would
take to have two increases.

20:10.896 --> 20:16.586 align:middle
So, you know, the Committee
as a whole never discusses,

20:16.586 --> 20:21.046 align:middle
how many increases should we
have this year or next year--

20:21.046 --> 20:25.356 align:middle
that's not a decision we're
making as a Committee.

20:25.916 --> 20:29.416 align:middle
We're making decisions on
a meeting-by-meeting basis

20:29.536 --> 20:34.516 align:middle
and trying to give a sense to
the public of what we're looking

20:34.516 --> 20:38.076 align:middle
for and what the basis
of a decision will be.

20:38.796 --> 20:41.616 align:middle
And, as I indicated,
first of all,

20:41.616 --> 20:46.826 align:middle
international uncertainties loom
large here; we mentioned Brexit,

20:46.876 --> 20:52.186 align:middle
the U.K. decision, obviously
how that turns out is something

20:52.186 --> 20:55.146 align:middle
that will factor into
future decisions.

20:55.536 --> 21:00.116 align:middle
And we're also looking at the
prospects for economic growth

21:00.496 --> 21:02.896 align:middle
and continued progress
in the labor market.

21:02.896 --> 21:07.906 align:middle
The forecast that
you see in the SEP

21:07.906 --> 21:11.366 align:middle
and the statement indicate
the Committee continues

21:11.366 --> 21:15.816 align:middle
to expect we will have moderate
growth, 2 percent growth--

21:15.816 --> 21:20.646 align:middle
so, you know, suggests healthy
growth for the rest of the year

21:20.646 --> 21:25.416 align:middle
and a pickup in growth in the
second quarter, and we expect

21:25.416 --> 21:28.806 align:middle
to see continuing progress
in the labor market.

21:29.366 --> 21:33.296 align:middle
Now, we had questions
about the growth outlook

21:33.396 --> 21:36.926 align:middle
because we did see slower
growth in the fourth quarter

21:36.926 --> 21:38.146 align:middle
and in the first quarter.

21:38.626 --> 21:42.356 align:middle
I have to say there, with
respect to the slowdown we saw

21:42.356 --> 21:44.876 align:middle
in consumer spending,
that seemed to be

21:44.876 --> 21:46.786 align:middle
out of line with fundamentals.

21:47.136 --> 21:51.016 align:middle
We expected it to pick up, and
we've seen very good evidence

21:51.016 --> 21:52.316 align:middle
that it has picked up.

21:52.706 --> 21:56.676 align:middle
But now, the labor market
appears to have slowed down,

21:57.106 --> 22:02.996 align:middle
and we need to assure ourselves
that the underlying momentum

22:02.996 --> 22:06.346 align:middle
in the economy has
not diminished.

22:06.836 --> 22:10.236 align:middle
So, as I said, we will be
carefully assessing data

22:10.236 --> 22:17.566 align:middle
on the labor market to make
sure that job gains are going

22:17.656 --> 22:22.976 align:middle
to continue at a pace
sufficient to result

22:22.976 --> 22:25.076 align:middle
in further improvement
in the labor market.

22:25.076 --> 22:27.576 align:middle
And we will be watching
the spending data

22:27.576 --> 22:30.086 align:middle
to make sure growth
is picking up in line

22:30.316 --> 22:31.566 align:middle
with our expectations.

22:31.806 --> 22:33.826 align:middle
Of course, with respect
to inflation,

22:34.866 --> 22:37.706 align:middle
we're constantly
evaluating whether

22:37.706 --> 22:41.536 align:middle
or not incoming information
is roughly in line

22:41.536 --> 22:42.976 align:middle
with our expectations.

22:42.976 --> 22:46.506 align:middle
So we will be evaluating
that at every meeting;

22:46.506 --> 22:50.156 align:middle
every meeting is live, and
we could make a decision

22:50.156 --> 22:55.486 align:middle
at any meeting to adjust the
funds rate, but that's the kind

22:55.486 --> 22:58.386 align:middle
of thing that we will want to
see to make such decisions.

22:59.676 --> 23:00.796 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

23:01.656 --> 23:05.386 align:middle
The Fed created a labor market
conditions index a couple

23:05.386 --> 23:09.956 align:middle
of years ago that was designed
to sort of bring together a lot

23:09.956 --> 23:11.876 align:middle
of these factors in the labor
market that you've talked about.

23:11.916 --> 23:14.866 align:middle
As I'm sure you know, it's
been falling since January.

23:15.226 --> 23:17.626 align:middle
That suggests to some people
that it was your decision

23:17.626 --> 23:19.096 align:middle
to raise rates in December

23:19.256 --> 23:21.426 align:middle
that has caused this
weakening in the labor market.

23:21.836 --> 23:23.086 align:middle
Could you address
what role, if any,

23:23.086 --> 23:25.866 align:middle
you think the Fed's decision
to raise rates has played

23:25.866 --> 23:27.366 align:middle
in the slowdown we
are now seeing?

23:27.366 --> 23:27.806 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

23:28.386 --> 23:31.806 align:middle
Well, let me just say the labor
market conditions index is a

23:31.806 --> 23:38.316 align:middle
kind of experimental research
product that's a summary measure

23:38.316 --> 23:42.116 align:middle
of many different
indicators, and, essentially,

23:42.446 --> 23:46.346 align:middle
that measure tries
to assess the change

23:46.346 --> 23:48.096 align:middle
in the labor market conditions.

23:49.276 --> 23:52.566 align:middle
As I look at it, and as
that index looks at things,

23:52.966 --> 23:55.526 align:middle
the state of the labor
market is still healthy,

23:56.076 --> 24:00.386 align:middle
but there's been something
of a loss of momentum:

24:00.706 --> 24:06.466 align:middle
The 200,000 jobs a month
we saw, for example,

24:06.466 --> 24:09.426 align:middle
in the first quarter
of the year,

24:09.856 --> 24:12.986 align:middle
that's slowed in recent months.

24:13.036 --> 24:15.936 align:middle
Exactly what the reasons
are for that slowing,

24:17.216 --> 24:18.486 align:middle
it's difficult to say.

24:18.486 --> 24:20.236 align:middle
It may turn out--
you know, again,

24:20.236 --> 24:23.826 align:middle
we should never pay too much
attention to, for example,

24:23.826 --> 24:25.376 align:middle
one job market report.

24:25.736 --> 24:27.506 align:middle
There's a large error
around that,

24:27.506 --> 24:29.586 align:middle
we often see large revisions,

24:30.476 --> 24:35.626 align:middle
we should not overblow the
significance of one data point,

24:35.846 --> 24:38.516 align:middle
especially when other indicators

24:38.516 --> 24:44.036 align:middle
of the labor market are still
flashing green: Initial claims

24:44.036 --> 24:47.236 align:middle
for unemployment
insurance remain low;

24:47.586 --> 24:54.586 align:middle
perceptions of the labor market
remain fine; data from the JOLTS

24:54.926 --> 24:58.596 align:middle
on job openings continue
to reach new highs.

24:58.886 --> 25:01.956 align:middle
So there's a good
deal of incoming data

25:02.066 --> 25:07.046 align:middle
that does signal continued
progress and strength

25:07.046 --> 25:10.756 align:middle
in the labor market, but, as
I say, it does bear watching.

25:11.036 --> 25:14.896 align:middle
So, the Committee doesn't
feel and doesn't expect,

25:14.896 --> 25:17.716 align:middle
and I don't expect,
that labor market--

25:17.826 --> 25:20.916 align:middle
progress in the labor
market has come to an end.

25:21.396 --> 25:24.496 align:middle
We have tried to make
clear to the public

25:24.706 --> 25:28.236 align:middle
and through our actions and
through the revisions you see--

25:28.446 --> 25:31.926 align:middle
have seen over time
in the dot plot--

25:32.316 --> 25:39.076 align:middle
that we do not have a fixed plan
for raising rates over time.

25:39.076 --> 25:43.276 align:middle
We look at incoming data and
are prepared to adjust our views

25:43.276 --> 25:45.996 align:middle
to keep the economy
on track, and,

25:46.156 --> 25:50.456 align:middle
in light of that data
dependence of our policy,

25:50.836 --> 25:55.486 align:middle
I really don't think that
a single rate increase

25:55.486 --> 26:00.466 align:middle
of 25 basis points
in December has any--

26:00.736 --> 26:03.826 align:middle
has had much significance
for the outlook.

26:04.236 --> 26:07.676 align:middle
And we will continue to
adjust our thinking in light

26:07.676 --> 26:10.396 align:middle
of incoming data in whatever
direction is appropriate.

26:11.786 --> 26:13.486 align:middle
JON HILSENRATH.

26:16.016 --> 26:17.576 align:middle
Chair Yellen, I want
to come back

26:17.576 --> 26:19.216 align:middle
to these longer-run
rate projections

26:19.366 --> 26:20.736 align:middle
that you've been asked about.

26:20.736 --> 26:25.676 align:middle
So, yields on 10-year Treasury
notes have fallen below 1.6

26:25.726 --> 26:28.946 align:middle
percent; on 5-year notes,
they're near 1 percent.

26:29.616 --> 26:32.786 align:middle
Elsewhere in the world,
in Germany and Japan,

26:32.786 --> 26:34.866 align:middle
long-term bond yields
are negative.

26:35.356 --> 26:38.526 align:middle
Does-- how do you
explain this low level

26:38.526 --> 26:39.886 align:middle
of long-term bond yields?

26:39.946 --> 26:44.686 align:middle
And does it give you
any pause in looking

26:44.686 --> 26:47.796 align:middle
at your own projections
and coming to a conclusion

26:47.796 --> 26:51.136 align:middle
about whether those projections
are possibly still way too high

26:51.316 --> 26:54.986 align:middle
when the bond market is
at a much lower level?

26:55.246 --> 26:55.716 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

26:55.716 --> 26:58.116 align:middle
So I think the levels

26:58.116 --> 27:02.746 align:middle
of longer-term rates reflect
essentially two things.

27:03.236 --> 27:07.836 align:middle
One is market expectations
about the path

27:07.836 --> 27:11.266 align:middle
of short-term rates over,
if we're considering, say,

27:11.266 --> 27:15.816 align:middle
a 10-year Treasury security,
what would be the path

27:16.466 --> 27:19.396 align:middle
of short-term rates
over the next 10 years.

27:19.896 --> 27:24.786 align:middle
And the second factor is
the so-called term premium,

27:24.786 --> 27:30.816 align:middle
or the extra yield that
investors demand in order

27:30.876 --> 27:36.076 align:middle
to hold a longer-term security
instead of to invest short term.

27:36.786 --> 27:40.516 align:middle
And clearly, market
expectations for the path

27:40.516 --> 27:42.756 align:middle
of short-term interest rates

27:43.006 --> 27:49.136 align:middle
over the next 10 years remain
low, and that is a factor.

27:49.136 --> 27:52.406 align:middle
That is an important
factor that's, I think,

27:52.406 --> 27:55.426 align:middle
holding down the level
of longer-term yields.

27:55.826 --> 28:00.646 align:middle
But, perhaps as important,
or maybe even more important,

28:00.936 --> 28:06.806 align:middle
the term premium is also low
and has probably come down.

28:07.226 --> 28:12.216 align:middle
Now, when we engaged in
longer-term asset purchases,

28:12.616 --> 28:18.066 align:middle
our very purpose in
doing that was to drive

28:18.126 --> 28:23.446 align:middle
down longer-term yields by
making these assets scarce--

28:23.606 --> 28:28.666 align:middle
scarcer and, hence, more
valuable to the public

28:28.716 --> 28:32.076 align:middle
that wants to invest in
long-term securities,

28:32.396 --> 28:35.456 align:middle
and we were consciously
attempting to drive

28:35.526 --> 28:36.916 align:middle
down that term premium.

28:37.376 --> 28:41.186 align:middle
Now, we continue to hold a large
quantity of those securities,

28:41.186 --> 28:43.146 align:middle
but we're not adding to them.

28:43.376 --> 28:47.656 align:middle
But, in many parts of the
world-- the ECB, for example,

28:47.656 --> 28:52.556 align:middle
and the Bank of Japan are also
engaging in quantitative easing,

28:52.556 --> 28:57.396 align:middle
buying longer-term assets and
pushing down those term premia.

28:57.676 --> 29:00.706 align:middle
So I think term premia
are very low as well

29:00.706 --> 29:02.546 align:middle
as the expected path
of short rates.

29:02.546 --> 29:02.926 align:middle
JON HILSENRATH.

29:02.926 --> 29:05.476 align:middle
Do these yield levels
give you any uncertainty,

29:05.606 --> 29:07.296 align:middle
any doubt about whether--

29:07.296 --> 29:08.806 align:middle
are you going to be
able to get rates

29:08.966 --> 29:11.066 align:middle
to where projections
say they're going?

29:12.236 --> 29:12.616 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

29:12.616 --> 29:18.496 align:middle
Well, so I want to say
again, we're quite uncertain

29:18.556 --> 29:23.466 align:middle
about where rates are
heading in the longer term.

29:24.856 --> 29:30.976 align:middle
We write down our best
estimates at this time

29:31.396 --> 29:36.296 align:middle
of what is a longer-run normal
level of the federal funds rate,

29:38.876 --> 29:41.156 align:middle
and those are numbers

29:41.156 --> 29:44.106 align:middle
about which there is
great uncertainty.

29:44.626 --> 29:48.786 align:middle
As I said, we have
good reason to believe

29:48.876 --> 29:51.926 align:middle
that the so-called neutral
rate or rate compatible

29:51.926 --> 29:55.076 align:middle
with the economy operating

29:55.076 --> 29:58.906 align:middle
at full employment is
low at the present time.

29:59.486 --> 30:05.656 align:middle
And many of us believe, as
a base case, it's reasonable

30:05.656 --> 30:08.676 align:middle
to assume that those rates
will move up over time,

30:09.256 --> 30:10.576 align:middle
but we're not certain of that.

30:11.156 --> 30:13.386 align:middle
It is-- it's one of the
uncertainties that--

30:13.646 --> 30:18.376 align:middle
and there could be revisions in
either direction, but thus far,

30:19.336 --> 30:25.406 align:middle
in recent SEPs, I'd say the
revisions have mainly been--

30:25.916 --> 30:27.936 align:middle
have been in the
downward direction.

30:28.736 --> 30:32.226 align:middle
The idea that a low
neutral rate may be more--

30:32.636 --> 30:36.646 align:middle
closer to the new normal, but
you still do see some reversion.

30:36.646 --> 30:39.816 align:middle
So we're really quite
uncertain about that.

30:41.036 --> 30:45.186 align:middle
YLAN MUI. In your
speech in Philadelphia,

30:45.186 --> 30:48.706 align:middle
you called the slowdown in job
growth last month "concerning,"

30:49.086 --> 30:51.346 align:middle
and you mentioned today
that you want to verify

30:51.346 --> 30:52.966 align:middle
that the underlying
momentum in the economy

30:52.966 --> 30:55.236 align:middle
and in the labor market
is still continuing.

30:55.626 --> 30:58.196 align:middle
What do you need to
see to convince you

30:58.466 --> 31:01.656 align:middle
that the labor market is still
moving toward full employment,

31:01.656 --> 31:03.136 align:middle
and for how long would
you need to see it?

31:03.136 --> 31:03.396 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

31:03.716 --> 31:08.096 align:middle
So I can't give you a formula,
I know you would probably

31:08.176 --> 31:12.606 align:middle
like to have a number that's a
cutoff for what we need to see

31:12.606 --> 31:14.146 align:middle
in a particular report.

31:14.606 --> 31:19.666 align:middle
There are a lot of different
indicators of the labor market--

31:19.666 --> 31:23.406 align:middle
for example, the labor
market conditions index

31:23.406 --> 31:29.386 align:middle
that Binya referred to has
19 different indicators.

31:30.066 --> 31:33.606 align:middle
Clearly, we will be looking
at the next job report,

31:33.606 --> 31:40.376 align:middle
and if we were to see a healthy
pace of job growth, you know,

31:40.376 --> 31:43.356 align:middle
above that needed to kind

31:43.356 --> 31:46.236 align:middle
of maintain the status
quo in the labor market.

31:46.236 --> 31:50.286 align:middle
So, you know, I should say, over
time, we should expect to see,

31:50.596 --> 31:55.876 align:middle
as the economy comes closer
to maximum employment,

31:56.436 --> 32:00.506 align:middle
the likely pace of job
gains is probably going

32:00.506 --> 32:02.056 align:middle
to slow down somewhat.

32:02.686 --> 32:04.686 align:middle
And we have seen some slowing.

32:04.686 --> 32:09.326 align:middle
But the recent couple of months
was very low and, arguably,

32:09.326 --> 32:11.886 align:middle
not even at the pace
we need to see

32:11.886 --> 32:14.986 align:middle
to maintain stable
labor market conditions.

32:15.036 --> 32:20.116 align:middle
So we want to see an
adequate pace of job creation.

32:20.116 --> 32:22.956 align:middle
There might be revisions
to previous months

32:22.956 --> 32:26.686 align:middle
that would change our views,
but there will be other surveys

32:27.256 --> 32:31.576 align:middle
of employment intentions
and other indicators

32:31.576 --> 32:35.966 align:middle
of the labor market that we'll
focus-- we'll be focusing on.

32:35.966 --> 32:38.596 align:middle
So, there is no formula
for what it takes,

32:38.646 --> 32:40.936 align:middle
but we will be looking
at the labor market.

32:41.886 --> 32:45.186 align:middle
Did you want to follow up?

32:45.276 --> 32:47.276 align:middle
YLAN MUI. Sorry, I
had a quick follow-up.

32:47.276 --> 32:49.616 align:middle
Also in your speech in
Philadelphia, you did not say

32:49.616 --> 32:51.796 align:middle
that you felt that it would
be probably appropriate

32:52.086 --> 32:55.896 align:middle
for a rate hike to occur
in the coming months.

32:56.706 --> 33:00.946 align:middle
Do you-- did you
intentionally leave that out?

33:01.616 --> 33:02.386 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:02.386 --> 33:03.436 align:middle
You know, we do need
to make sure

33:03.436 --> 33:05.176 align:middle
that there is sufficient
momentum.

33:05.596 --> 33:08.926 align:middle
I don't know what the
timetable is going to be

33:09.026 --> 33:10.806 align:middle
to gain that assurance.

33:11.216 --> 33:14.986 align:middle
Every meeting is live;
there is no meeting

33:14.986 --> 33:18.206 align:middle
that is off the table, that--

33:18.386 --> 33:22.386 align:middle
no meeting is out in terms
of a possible rate increase.

33:23.146 --> 33:26.196 align:middle
But, we really need
to look at the data,

33:26.196 --> 33:29.826 align:middle
and I can't prespecify
a timetable.

33:29.826 --> 33:34.026 align:middle
So I'm, you know, not
comfortable to say it's

33:34.026 --> 33:36.626 align:middle
in the next meeting or
two, but it could be,

33:36.896 --> 33:38.436 align:middle
it could be, it's
not impossible.

33:38.816 --> 33:41.516 align:middle
It's not impossible that
by July, for example,

33:41.846 --> 33:46.606 align:middle
we would see data that led
us to believe that we're

33:46.606 --> 33:50.426 align:middle
on a perfectly fine course, and
that data was an aberration,

33:50.856 --> 33:53.126 align:middle
and other concerns
would have passed.

33:54.956 --> 33:55.546 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

33:55.546 --> 33:56.806 align:middle
Ma'am, Peter Barnes,
Fox Business.

33:57.346 --> 34:00.256 align:middle
Hi. We are in an election
season, and in the past,

34:00.316 --> 34:02.296 align:middle
the Fed has been sensitive

34:02.556 --> 34:05.786 align:middle
to making policy changes
in election years.

34:06.796 --> 34:09.446 align:middle
You have three more
meetings before the November

34:09.566 --> 34:10.536 align:middle
presidential election.

34:11.086 --> 34:15.596 align:middle
Could you comment on whether
or not the election will come

34:15.686 --> 34:20.996 align:middle
into play, and any concern
that if you change policy ahead

34:20.996 --> 34:24.116 align:middle
of the election and, based
on your forecast today,

34:24.666 --> 34:28.336 align:middle
you obviously could--
are you concerned

34:28.336 --> 34:31.656 align:middle
that that could then lead to
charges that the Fed is trying

34:31.656 --> 34:34.726 align:middle
to change policy to influence
the outcome of the election,

34:35.106 --> 34:39.016 align:middle
because the Fed has been
sensitive to that in the past?

34:39.396 --> 34:39.656 align:middle
Thank you.

34:39.656 --> 34:40.226 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

34:40.776 --> 34:45.626 align:middle
So we are very focused on
assessing the economic outlook

34:46.016 --> 34:48.806 align:middle
and making changes
that are appropriate

34:49.306 --> 34:53.326 align:middle
without taking politics
into account.

34:53.786 --> 34:58.326 align:middle
Look, if the data-- incoming
data were, in the coming months,

34:58.926 --> 35:04.276 align:middle
to justify the kind
of gradual increases

35:04.276 --> 35:08.566 align:middle
that we have long discussed,
that we see as appropriate

35:09.186 --> 35:14.806 align:middle
in light of the outlook, I think
markets should not be surprised

35:14.836 --> 35:16.996 align:middle
by such a decision
if we make it.

35:17.306 --> 35:21.966 align:middle
And it's obviously consistent
with the data that we've seen,

35:22.316 --> 35:26.256 align:middle
and the Committee will feel free
to move in the coming months

35:26.446 --> 35:27.776 align:middle
if we think it's appropriate.

35:29.136 --> 35:32.806 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

35:32.806 --> 35:33.816 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek, Bloomberg.

35:34.346 --> 35:36.066 align:middle
You mentioned in your
remarks at the beginning

35:36.066 --> 35:38.276 align:middle
that we are getting a slightly
different signal when you look

35:38.276 --> 35:41.016 align:middle
at inflation versus when you
look at inflation expectations.

35:41.476 --> 35:43.826 align:middle
Could you detail a little
bit which you look at

35:43.826 --> 35:44.936 align:middle
and sort of weight more?

35:44.936 --> 35:47.426 align:middle
Are you more concerned with
the inflation expectations

35:47.426 --> 35:50.536 align:middle
or focusing more on the slight
pickup in actual inflation?

35:50.736 --> 35:51.346 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

35:51.346 --> 35:53.756 align:middle
Well, we're looking at both.

35:54.406 --> 35:57.096 align:middle
You know, I would say, with
respect to the behavior

35:57.096 --> 36:02.086 align:middle
of inflation, inflation
is behaving roughly

36:02.086 --> 36:04.056 align:middle
in the manner I would
have expected;

36:04.056 --> 36:08.076 align:middle
I have really not seen
significant surprises there.

36:08.446 --> 36:11.336 align:middle
We've long said that
an important reason

36:11.336 --> 36:16.856 align:middle
that inflation is as low as it's
been is because of past declines

36:16.856 --> 36:22.106 align:middle
in energy prices and increases
in the value of the dollar,

36:22.406 --> 36:26.166 align:middle
and as those factors
began to dissipate,

36:26.166 --> 36:28.056 align:middle
we would see inflation
moving up.

36:28.396 --> 36:30.956 align:middle
Now, that's exactly what
we're seeing, what we're--

36:31.426 --> 36:34.116 align:middle
that's in line with our
thinking and with the data.

36:34.526 --> 36:36.386 align:middle
So, those things
have stabilized,

36:36.806 --> 36:38.776 align:middle
their influence is dissipating.

36:39.206 --> 36:43.086 align:middle
And with respect to
core inflation, which--

36:43.086 --> 36:45.916 align:middle
now, that's partly
influenced also by the dollar,

36:45.916 --> 36:47.996 align:middle
but trying to pull
out the dollar

36:47.996 --> 36:50.196 align:middle
and import price influence--

36:50.426 --> 36:54.396 align:middle
core inflation seems
to be behaving roughly

36:54.396 --> 36:55.746 align:middle
as one would expect

36:56.076 --> 36:59.266 align:middle
with well-anchored
inflation expectations

36:59.646 --> 37:02.296 align:middle
and in improving labor markets.

37:02.296 --> 37:06.986 align:middle
So I'm not seeing anything--
inflation, even core inflation,

37:06.986 --> 37:08.516 align:middle
is running under 2 percent.

37:08.896 --> 37:13.446 align:middle
I continue to think the
evidence supports a projection

37:13.446 --> 37:15.426 align:middle
that it will move up
over the next couple

37:15.426 --> 37:19.136 align:middle
of years back toward
our 2 percent objective.

37:19.586 --> 37:25.616 align:middle
But, we've seen in the past,
and economic theory suggests,

37:25.936 --> 37:29.986 align:middle
that inflation expectations
are relevant to price

37:29.986 --> 37:31.966 align:middle
and wage setting decisions.

37:32.356 --> 37:34.906 align:middle
So we do monitor indicators

37:34.906 --> 37:37.676 align:middle
of inflation expectations
carefully.

37:38.466 --> 37:41.866 align:middle
Now, it's very hard to
know exactly what inflation

37:41.866 --> 37:46.236 align:middle
expectations are relevant to
actual price and wage decisions.

37:46.616 --> 37:50.716 align:middle
And so, for example, we have
seen the Michigan survey,

37:50.956 --> 37:57.626 align:middle
a measure of household inflation
expectations, move down.

37:57.756 --> 38:00.006 align:middle
It's hard-- that's a
preliminary number,

38:00.086 --> 38:01.946 align:middle
it's hard to know
what to make of it.

38:02.156 --> 38:04.936 align:middle
We've certainly taken
note of it.

38:05.336 --> 38:10.776 align:middle
But survey-based measures that--
where forecasters are queried--

38:10.776 --> 38:12.796 align:middle
have really all been
quite stable.

38:13.486 --> 38:16.196 align:middle
And measures of inflation
compensation--

38:16.286 --> 38:18.796 align:middle
I always try to be
careful to call it

38:18.796 --> 38:22.846 align:middle
"inflation compensation" rather
than "inflation expectations"

38:23.186 --> 38:25.856 align:middle
because they're not
inflation expectations.

38:26.226 --> 38:29.936 align:middle
Inflation expectations
influence those market measures,

38:30.306 --> 38:33.056 align:middle
but there's also an
inflation risk premium.

38:33.446 --> 38:36.096 align:middle
And there are actually
good reasons to think

38:36.096 --> 38:39.896 align:middle
that the inflation risk
premium could have declined

38:39.896 --> 38:42.966 align:middle
significantly and may be
depressing those measures.

38:43.256 --> 38:48.316 align:middle
So we watch them, we've
taken note in the statement

38:48.316 --> 38:51.086 align:middle
that they've moved down,

38:51.916 --> 38:55.906 align:middle
but actual inflation
is behaving more

38:55.906 --> 38:57.386 align:middle
or less as would be expected.

38:58.526 --> 39:01.906 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

39:01.906 --> 39:03.736 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

39:04.096 --> 39:06.316 align:middle
When the April minutes
were released,

39:06.316 --> 39:08.876 align:middle
they caught markets by surprise.

39:09.386 --> 39:10.896 align:middle
In there, they showed--

39:10.936 --> 39:13.156 align:middle
they seemed to show that
there was an active discussion

39:13.536 --> 39:15.966 align:middle
of a possible June
rate increase,

39:16.296 --> 39:18.926 align:middle
something that we hadn't gotten
from the policy statement

39:19.196 --> 39:20.616 align:middle
that was issued right
after the meeting.

39:20.616 --> 39:25.026 align:middle
Was that a conscious decision
to hold back and tell us in--

39:25.296 --> 39:29.156 align:middle
when the minutes came out
about the June discussion?

39:29.556 --> 39:32.916 align:middle
And if so, could you tell us
what surprises we could see

39:32.916 --> 39:33.676 align:middle
in the June minutes?

39:34.106 --> 39:34.416 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:35.766 --> 39:41.316 align:middle
So the minutes are always-- have
to be an accurate discussion

39:41.556 --> 39:43.336 align:middle
of what happened at the meeting.

39:43.696 --> 39:48.266 align:middle
So they're not changed
after the fact in order

39:48.266 --> 39:50.976 align:middle
to correct possible
misconceptions.

39:50.976 --> 39:55.306 align:middle
There was a good deal of
discussion at that meeting

39:55.536 --> 39:59.136 align:middle
of the possibility
of moving in June,

39:59.136 --> 40:01.546 align:middle
and that appeared
in the minutes.

40:01.726 --> 40:05.096 align:middle
I suppose in the
April statement,

40:05.096 --> 40:12.146 align:middle
we gave no obvious hint or
kind of calendar-based signal

40:12.216 --> 40:16.036 align:middle
that June was a possibility.

40:16.446 --> 40:19.456 align:middle
But I think if you
look at the statement,

40:20.126 --> 40:22.996 align:middle
we pointed to slower
growth but pointed

40:22.996 --> 40:24.576 align:middle
out that the fundamentals--

40:24.576 --> 40:27.316 align:middle
there was no obvious
fundamental reason

40:27.316 --> 40:28.996 align:middle
for growth to have slowed.

40:29.446 --> 40:34.996 align:middle
And we pointed to fundamentals
underlying household spending

40:34.996 --> 40:38.056 align:middle
decisions that remained
on solid ground,

40:38.306 --> 40:41.776 align:middle
suggesting that maybe this
was something transitory

40:41.866 --> 40:44.126 align:middle
that would disappear.

40:44.126 --> 40:48.496 align:middle
We noted that labor market
conditions continued to improve

40:48.806 --> 40:50.786 align:middle
in line with our expectations,

40:51.166 --> 40:57.136 align:middle
and we did downgrade somewhat
our expressions of concern

40:57.136 --> 41:00.436 align:middle
about the global
risk environment.

41:00.436 --> 41:06.526 align:middle
So I do think that there were
hints in the April statement

41:06.616 --> 41:10.506 align:middle
that the Committee
was changing its views

41:10.506 --> 41:12.446 align:middle
of what it was seeing
in a direction.

41:12.756 --> 41:15.876 align:middle
We continue to say
that we think,

41:15.876 --> 41:21.506 align:middle
if economic developments evolve
in line with our expectations,

41:21.796 --> 41:26.226 align:middle
the gradual and cautious
further increases we expect

41:26.226 --> 41:27.446 align:middle
to be appropriate.

41:27.776 --> 41:31.776 align:middle
And I suppose I was
somewhat surprised

41:31.776 --> 41:34.036 align:middle
with the market interpretation
of it.

41:34.306 --> 41:36.516 align:middle
But the June meeting minutes--

41:36.516 --> 41:41.796 align:middle
the minutes of the April
meeting were an accurate summary

41:41.796 --> 41:43.326 align:middle
of what had happened.

41:44.296 --> 41:47.876 align:middle
JEREMY TORDJMAN.

41:47.876 --> 41:50.306 align:middle
Jeremy Tordjman with
the AFP news agency.

41:50.306 --> 41:54.616 align:middle
The Fed has repeatedly voiced
its concern over the slow pace

41:54.616 --> 41:57.456 align:middle
of wage growth, and I was
wondering, do you think

41:57.456 --> 42:00.666 align:middle
that increasing the federal
minimum wage could be

42:00.666 --> 42:01.536 align:middle
of any help?

42:01.586 --> 42:03.836 align:middle
Could it boost the higher wages

42:03.836 --> 42:06.356 align:middle
and eventually drive
up the inflation?

42:06.926 --> 42:08.586 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

42:08.586 --> 42:12.526 align:middle
So I think that the minimum
wage increases that have gone

42:12.526 --> 42:14.976 align:middle
into effect-- estimates

42:14.976 --> 42:20.676 align:middle
that I've seen suggest it's
a relatively minor influence

42:20.676 --> 42:24.256 align:middle
on the aggregate level
of wage inflation.

42:24.766 --> 42:31.366 align:middle
I would take somewhat faster
wage increases to be a sign

42:31.366 --> 42:34.066 align:middle
that labor market
slack is diminishing

42:34.436 --> 42:38.466 align:middle
and that the labor market
is approaching conditions

42:38.466 --> 42:41.496 align:middle
that are consistent
with maximum employment.

42:41.876 --> 42:49.156 align:middle
So, I think, you know, I
think we have seen some hints,

42:49.206 --> 42:52.966 align:middle
perhaps preliminary indications,
that wage growth is picking up.

42:53.436 --> 42:56.966 align:middle
And as much as anything,
I think it's a sign

42:57.026 --> 42:59.456 align:middle
of a generally healthy
labor market,

42:59.686 --> 43:03.586 align:middle
which is what our
mandated objective is,

43:03.586 --> 43:05.736 align:middle
to achieve maximum employment.

43:05.956 --> 43:08.946 align:middle
And so it would be
a symptom of it.

43:09.056 --> 43:10.986 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

43:14.116 --> 43:15.366 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

43:16.036 --> 43:19.366 align:middle
There's been a lot of
discussion in last couple months

43:19.366 --> 43:23.726 align:middle
about the slow pace of
demand in the global economy,

43:23.946 --> 43:27.746 align:middle
and some economists think that
central banks should think

43:27.746 --> 43:29.236 align:middle
about using helicopter money,

43:29.816 --> 43:33.056 align:middle
maybe in Japan first
or Europe first.

43:33.436 --> 43:36.906 align:middle
But then, former Fed Chairman
Ben Bernanke weighed in saying

43:36.906 --> 43:39.366 align:middle
that he thought it would
be a good thing for the Fed

43:39.366 --> 43:41.496 align:middle
to put helicopter
money in its toolkit

43:41.906 --> 43:44.096 align:middle
in case there was a downturn
in the United States.

43:44.586 --> 43:46.186 align:middle
So I'd like to get
your comments on that.

43:46.506 --> 43:46.826 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:47.546 --> 43:52.046 align:middle
So, in normal times, I
think it's very important

43:52.046 --> 43:55.226 align:middle
that there be a separation
between monetary

43:55.226 --> 44:00.596 align:middle
and fiscal policy, and
it's a primary reason

44:00.596 --> 44:03.296 align:middle
for independence
of a central bank.

44:03.836 --> 44:10.946 align:middle
We have seen all too many
examples of countries that end

44:10.946 --> 44:17.006 align:middle
up with high or even
hyperinflation because those

44:17.006 --> 44:20.896 align:middle
in charge of fiscal policy
direct their central bank

44:21.256 --> 44:24.186 align:middle
to help them finance
it by printing money,

44:24.656 --> 44:29.786 align:middle
and maintaining price
stability and low

44:29.786 --> 44:33.566 align:middle
and stable inflation
is very much aided

44:33.616 --> 44:36.016 align:middle
by having central
bank independence.

44:36.676 --> 44:43.526 align:middle
Now, that said, in unusual
times where the concern is

44:43.526 --> 44:46.916 align:middle
with very weak growth
or possibly deflation--

44:47.396 --> 44:49.676 align:middle
rather rare circumstances--

44:49.676 --> 44:53.356 align:middle
first of all, fiscal policy
can be a very important tool.

44:53.916 --> 44:58.066 align:middle
And it's natural that if
it can be employed that,

44:58.226 --> 45:01.906 align:middle
just as monetary policy
is doing a lot to try

45:01.906 --> 45:05.716 align:middle
to stimulate growth, that fiscal
policy should play a role.

45:06.086 --> 45:09.186 align:middle
And normally, you would
hope, in an economy

45:09.186 --> 45:12.666 align:middle
with those severe
downside risks, monetary

45:12.666 --> 45:16.496 align:middle
and fiscal policy would not
be working at cross purposes

45:16.536 --> 45:17.816 align:middle
to get-- but together.

45:18.356 --> 45:22.136 align:middle
Now, whether or not in
such extreme circumstances,

45:22.636 --> 45:26.866 align:middle
there might be a case for,
let's say, coordination--

45:26.966 --> 45:31.006 align:middle
close coordination, with the
central bank playing a role

45:31.356 --> 45:34.686 align:middle
in financing fiscal
policy; this is something

45:34.686 --> 45:39.586 align:middle
that academics are
debating, and it is something

45:39.586 --> 45:42.326 align:middle
that one might legitimately
consider.

45:43.126 --> 45:49.696 align:middle
I would see this as a very
abnormal, extreme situation

45:50.166 --> 45:53.086 align:middle
where one needs an
all-out attempt,

45:53.086 --> 45:58.366 align:middle
and even then it's a matter
that academics are debating,

45:58.416 --> 46:00.906 align:middle
but only in an unusual
situation.

46:01.866 --> 46:03.276 align:middle
JUSTINE UNDERHILL.

46:04.376 --> 46:05.966 align:middle
Justine Underhill,
Yahoo Finance.

46:06.486 --> 46:09.536 align:middle
So now that the Fed has started
the process of raising rates,

46:10.056 --> 46:12.946 align:middle
various Fed officials have
said, including Ben Bernanke,

46:12.996 --> 46:16.496 align:middle
that the Fed could go cash
flow negative in this scenario

46:16.496 --> 46:21.116 align:middle
as capital losses are taken
on the portfolio of bonds.

46:21.166 --> 46:23.886 align:middle
Do you still see this happening,
and when might this happen?

46:23.886 --> 46:24.716 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:25.316 --> 46:27.586 align:middle
So you're talking about
our income going negative?

46:27.586 --> 46:28.186 align:middle
JUSTINE UNDERHILL.

46:28.636 --> 46:29.766 align:middle
Yes. CHAIR YELLEN.

46:29.836 --> 46:34.576 align:middle
Well, it is conceivable
in a scenario when--

46:34.706 --> 46:41.356 align:middle
where growth and inflation
really surprise us to the upside

46:41.886 --> 46:45.286 align:middle
that we would have to raise
short-term interest rates

46:45.286 --> 46:49.506 align:middle
so rapidly that the
rates we would be paying

46:49.876 --> 46:54.866 align:middle
on reserves would exceed what
we're earning on our portfolio.

46:55.156 --> 47:02.306 align:middle
Now even then, we have about
$2 trillion of liabilities,

47:02.306 --> 47:05.986 align:middle
namely currency on which
we pay no interest.

47:06.336 --> 47:10.526 align:middle
So this does requires
an extreme scenario

47:10.846 --> 47:15.406 align:middle
with very rapid increases in
short-term interest rates.

47:15.456 --> 47:21.476 align:middle
So it is conceivable but quite
unlikely that it could happen.

47:22.876 --> 47:27.906 align:middle
But, you know, if it were to
happen, we would have an economy

47:27.976 --> 47:29.536 align:middle
that would be doing very well.

47:29.596 --> 47:31.696 align:middle
This is probably an economy

47:32.056 --> 47:35.506 align:middle
that everybody would
feel very pleased--

47:35.756 --> 47:39.066 align:middle
was performing well and
better than expected,

47:39.596 --> 47:42.306 align:middle
and where monetary policy--

47:42.916 --> 47:45.096 align:middle
you know, our goal
is price stability

47:45.096 --> 47:48.046 align:middle
and maximum employment,
and we would probably feel

47:48.046 --> 47:50.636 align:middle
that we had done very
well in achieving that.

47:50.636 --> 47:55.106 align:middle
So, we usually make money--
we've been making a lot of money

47:55.106 --> 47:59.386 align:middle
in recent years, but the goal
of monetary policy is not

47:59.436 --> 48:01.826 align:middle
to maximize our income.

48:02.066 --> 48:05.016 align:middle
And, you know, in a very
strong economy like that,

48:05.366 --> 48:08.236 align:middle
the Treasury would be
seeing a lot of inflows

48:08.496 --> 48:10.956 align:middle
in the form of tax
revenues, too.

48:11.486 --> 48:14.646 align:middle
STEVE BECKNER.

48:15.196 --> 48:18.216 align:middle
Madam Chair, Steve Beckner
of Market News International.

48:18.216 --> 48:22.306 align:middle
To what extent do
you feel constrained

48:22.306 --> 48:27.626 align:middle
in raising interest rates by
the low or even negative rates

48:27.726 --> 48:31.016 align:middle
that foreign central
banks are pursuing,

48:31.396 --> 48:33.976 align:middle
possibly out of concern
for what it might mean

48:34.386 --> 48:36.646 align:middle
for the dollar exchange rate?

48:36.996 --> 48:40.576 align:middle
And, if that is a constraint,
to what extent are you--

48:40.576 --> 48:45.286 align:middle
might you also be concerned
about the impact, long range,

48:45.716 --> 48:48.016 align:middle
of low domestic rates

48:48.016 --> 48:51.576 align:middle
on possibly distorting
domestic markets?

48:53.636 --> 48:54.306 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:54.546 --> 48:56.656 align:middle
So the state of foreign
economies,

48:56.736 --> 49:02.446 align:middle
both their growth outlooks and
the stance of monetary policy,

49:02.896 --> 49:06.816 align:middle
those are factors that
influence the U.S. outlook

49:06.816 --> 49:11.376 align:middle
and influence the appropriate
stance of monetary policy.

49:11.696 --> 49:18.426 align:middle
So, of course, we do look at
foreign rates, the prospects--

49:18.476 --> 49:22.806 align:middle
and the prospects for
growth in those economies

49:23.076 --> 49:25.506 align:middle
in considering the
stance of policy.

49:26.276 --> 49:33.706 align:middle
Differentials between countries
in likely policy paths do tend

49:33.706 --> 49:37.376 align:middle
to spill over into
exchange rates--

49:37.706 --> 49:42.336 align:middle
that is a standard part of
how monetary policy works--

49:42.756 --> 49:46.576 align:middle
and a stronger dollar,

49:48.456 --> 49:51.706 align:middle
those have a both--
a depressing effect.

49:51.966 --> 49:56.396 align:middle
It creates channels through
which domestic demand

49:56.396 --> 49:57.296 align:middle
is depressed.

49:57.616 --> 50:03.746 align:middle
At the moment, net exports--
well, for quite some time

50:03.746 --> 50:07.146 align:middle
and probably going forward,
they will be somewhat of a drag

50:07.496 --> 50:10.636 align:middle
on U.S. growth, so
that's a factor

50:10.706 --> 50:12.926 align:middle
that we take into account.

50:13.336 --> 50:17.126 align:middle
And increases in the
dollar that we've seen

50:17.126 --> 50:21.736 align:middle
since mid-2014 have served to
push inflation down as well;

50:21.736 --> 50:23.426 align:middle
it can also have impacts

50:23.426 --> 50:26.186 align:middle
on commodity prices
that are relevant.

50:26.446 --> 50:31.396 align:middle
So, it's-- it is certainly
relevant to the stance

50:31.396 --> 50:35.816 align:middle
of U.S. monetary policy and
a factor, but when one says

50:35.816 --> 50:39.096 align:middle
"a constraint," I really
would not go so far

50:39.096 --> 50:42.786 align:middle
as to say it is a
constraint on monetary policy.

50:43.196 --> 50:49.046 align:middle
When we have an outlook for
continuing above-trend growth--

50:49.136 --> 50:53.176 align:middle
that if we held rates
absolutely flat, we have reason

50:53.176 --> 50:56.576 align:middle
to believe inflation would
overshoot our target--

50:57.076 --> 51:01.266 align:middle
we would see a case to
gradually raise rates over time.

51:01.776 --> 51:06.596 align:middle
At the moment, I think markets
do expect, and this is factored

51:06.596 --> 51:10.546 align:middle
into market prices, a
gradual path for rates

51:10.546 --> 51:12.016 align:middle
to increase over time.

51:12.016 --> 51:16.876 align:middle
But, for example, if we were to
see upside surprises to growth

51:16.876 --> 51:21.326 align:middle
and to inflation and had to
raise short-term rates faster,

51:21.416 --> 51:24.716 align:middle
thought we should, one
of the channels by which

51:24.716 --> 51:28.716 align:middle
that would work would be the
associated impact on the dollar.

51:28.716 --> 51:30.896 align:middle
That is a standard channel

51:30.946 --> 51:34.856 align:middle
through which the monetary
policy transmission mechanism

51:34.856 --> 51:37.036 align:middle
works, and we would
take it into account

51:37.236 --> 51:38.706 align:middle
and would not feel constrained,

51:38.706 --> 51:40.846 align:middle
but that would be part
of how it would work.

51:41.856 --> 51:43.106 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

51:44.396 --> 51:47.226 align:middle
How much do you-- oh,

51:47.436 --> 51:49.166 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

51:49.206 --> 51:53.156 align:middle
How much are you watching
oil prices and their impact

51:53.156 --> 51:55.636 align:middle
on inflation, and how that
could affect the timing

51:55.636 --> 51:58.916 align:middle
of future rate increases and how
much you might increase rates?

51:59.716 --> 52:00.456 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:00.456 --> 52:03.066 align:middle
Well, oil prices have had
many different effects

52:03.066 --> 52:06.686 align:middle
on the economy, and so
we've been watching oil

52:06.726 --> 52:07.946 align:middle
prices closely.

52:07.946 --> 52:15.636 align:middle
As you said, falling oil
prices pull down inflation.

52:15.636 --> 52:19.866 align:middle
You know, it takes
falling oil prices

52:20.416 --> 52:24.406 align:middle
to lower inflation
on a sustained basis.

52:24.406 --> 52:28.966 align:middle
Once they stabilize at
whatever level, their impact

52:28.966 --> 52:31.986 align:middle
on inflation dissipates
over time.

52:32.676 --> 52:35.686 align:middle
So we're beginning to
see that happening.

52:36.026 --> 52:39.166 align:middle
Not only have they stabilized,
they have moved up some,

52:39.496 --> 52:41.496 align:middle
and their inflation is--

52:41.686 --> 52:45.146 align:middle
their impact on inflation
is waning over time.

52:45.646 --> 52:51.546 align:middle
But oil prices have also had a
very substantial negative effect

52:51.546 --> 52:55.736 align:middle
on drilling and mining
activity that's led to weakness

52:55.736 --> 53:01.896 align:middle
in investment spending and
job loss in manufacturing and,

53:01.896 --> 53:04.266 align:middle
obviously, in the energy sector.

53:04.666 --> 53:08.236 align:middle
Now, you know, it has different
effects in different countries

53:08.236 --> 53:09.416 align:middle
and different sectors.

53:09.726 --> 53:12.896 align:middle
For American households,
it's been a boon.

53:13.306 --> 53:16.616 align:middle
We've estimated that
since mid-2014,

53:16.866 --> 53:22.526 align:middle
the decline in energy prices and
oil prices has probably resulted

53:22.526 --> 53:27.386 align:middle
in gains of about $1,400
per U.S. household,

53:27.706 --> 53:31.436 align:middle
and that's had an offsetting
positive impact on spending.

53:31.726 --> 53:34.006 align:middle
But in many countries
around the world

53:34.366 --> 53:37.536 align:middle
that are important
commodity exporters,

53:37.846 --> 53:40.146 align:middle
the decline we've seen

53:40.146 --> 53:43.326 align:middle
in oil prices has had
a depressing effect

53:43.566 --> 53:46.686 align:middle
on their growth,
their trade with us

53:46.686 --> 53:51.896 align:middle
and other trade partners,
and caused problems

53:51.896 --> 53:55.276 align:middle
that have had spillovers to
the global economy as well.

53:55.276 --> 53:57.266 align:middle
So it's a complicated picture.

53:58.446 --> 54:01.086 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

54:04.686 --> 54:07.386 align:middle
Thank you.

